.TH FLIGHTLABTEST 1
.SH NAME
flightlabtest \- check every Danby three-dimensional flight model written as a flightlab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flightlabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Flightlabtest
reads the
.B SPEC
constant out of every model under
.B /appl/danby
that calls
.BR flightlab->run ,
so that what is checked is the string that ships, and then checks that
the spec parses, that it has six states, that no rate is a NaN at the
start, and that the first three derivatives are exactly the last three
states.
.PP
That last is the whole shape of this family.
.IR Flightlab (2)
plots height against downrange and lateral against downrange, and stops
the flight when the third state crosses zero, so it is relying on the six
state lines meaning downrange, lateral, height and then those three
velocities in that order.  A spec that listed them in another order would
still integrate, still draw, and be wrong everywhere \(em and the cheap
way to catch it is that a position's rate has to BE its own velocity,
which is true by construction if the order is right and almost never true
if it is not.  It is checked at the model's own start and at eleven
pseudo-random states.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/flightlabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR profilelabtest (1),
.IR trajlabtest (1),
.IR ecolabtest (1),
.IR flightlab (2)
.SH BUGS
Nothing here checks the accelerations, only the kinematics.  A wrong drag
coefficient draws a wrong flight that this test calls correct.
